    title: 'System health: what is running out, and what has quietly stopped arriving',
    what: 'A page for you alone that answers three questions before any of them becomes a problem.<br><br><b>What is running out.</b> Four meters, each a figure against its ceiling with a word as well as a colour. The <b>main database</b> is the one that matters — if it fills, the whole app stops writing, not one feature — and because nothing can measure it in a single step it is measured when you ask and the answer kept, which is what lets the page tell you how fast it is growing and roughly how many days are left. <b>Commands this month</b> are now counted exactly rather than guessed, and projected to month end: being 40% through the allowance on day 10 is a problem the running total alone does not show.<br><br><b>What has quietly stopped.</b> One line per outside connection. The one worth reading every time is the <b>Duda webhook</b> — a webhook that was never registered, or that stopped, looks <i>exactly</i> like a slow week from inside the app: comments stop appearing and nothing says why.<br><br><b>What needs attention.</b> A band at the top that is empty most of the time, and is therefore worth reading when it is not.<br><br>One honest gap: enquiries <b>read</b> from the analysis database are charged as well as written, and only the provider’s own dashboard knows that figure. The page says so rather than pretending the number it has is the whole bill.',
