    title: 'Lead analysis: what the enquiries say once there are enough of them to ask',
    what: 'A new <b>Lead analysis</b> page, and enquiries now live somewhere that can be asked questions rather than only fetched.<br><br><b>Junk and real.</b> Every enquiry is sorted as it arrives \u2014 somebody wanting their car done, or somebody selling SEO to the shop. What the team has taught it comes first, then rules, then the AI for the few that are genuinely unclear. Nothing is ever deleted: a false negative wastes a minute, a false positive loses a job nobody hears about, so it leans towards calling an enquiry real and lets you disagree. <b>Mark as junk</b> or <b>real</b> on any enquiry, and teach it the phrase or the sender while you are there.<br><br><b>Forms gone quiet.</b> A form that quietly breaks looks exactly like a slow month. This lists websites that used to get enquiries and have stopped \u2014 counting <b>real</b> ones only, so a form still collecting spam while the customers stopped still shows up.<br><br><b>Across all websites.</b> Which kinds of page produce enquiries, where they come from, what day and hour people get in touch \u2014 counts only, nobody named, and a row needs at least three websites behind it. No single client can see this; it comes from holding hundreds of websites rather than one.<br><br><b>Blasts.</b> The same message sent to several clients. One shop sees an odd email; across the whole list it is obvious.',
