    title: 'You choose what you get told about, and on which channel',
    what: 'Until now everyone got everything, everywhere. If Duda comment notifications weren’t your job, the only way to stop them was to stop reading the bell — which also meant missing the things that <b>were</b> yours.<br><br><b>Your account</b> now has a small grid: the kinds of notification down the side — <b>mentions and replies</b>, <b>work assigned to you</b>, <b>Duda comments waiting</b>, <b>scans finishing</b>, <b>false alarm reports</b>, <b>feature suggestions</b> — and the ways they can reach you across the top: <b>Bell</b>, <b>Pop-up</b>, <b>Slack</b>, <b>Email</b>. Untick any square.<br><br>It’s per channel on purpose, because usually it’s one channel that grates rather than the whole subject. You can keep Duda comments on the bell for when you go looking, and stop them interrupting you on Slack.<br><br><b>Everything is on unless you turn it off</b>, so nothing changes until you change it — and anything added to the app later starts out reaching everybody, because a new notification going quietly to nobody is the worse mistake. Your choices only affect <b>you</b>. Nobody else’s notifications change, and nobody is told what you switched off.<br><br>One exception: <b>a mention always reaches your bell.</b> You can mute the Slack message, the pop-up and the email for mentions, but the bell row is always written — so a teammate who tags you and waits is never waiting on somebody who had quietly switched themselves off. That square reads <b>Always</b> rather than a tick box.',
