    title: 'A renamed Google listing stops being amber, and a repeated photo says where it is',
    what: '<b>The map.</b> Google draws a map embed from a <b>place ID</b> and shows whatever that place is called <b>today</b>. The name sitting in the link — <span class="mono">!2sAMDetails - Car Care Products &amp; Detailing Shop - Elgin, Moray</span> — is only a label from the day somebody generated it, and <b>no visitor ever sees it</b>. So when the pin is in the client’s own town under an old name, that is a renamed listing and there is nothing on the page to fix. It was still amber, which reads as work, so the same item kept coming back after every rescan. It is now <b>info</b>, and the note says outright that nothing on the page is wrong. Regenerate the embed if you want the link tidy — or mark the old name <b>correct for this website</b> and it never returns.<br><br>A pin in a <b>different</b> town is still a warning to open and check, and a map with <b>no place ID</b> at all is still critical, because there the name really is what chooses the pin.<br><br><b>The photos.</b> "Same photo used in 3 places" raised exactly one question — <b>which three?</b> — and the answer was only inside the item. The places are now on the audit row itself: the part of the page, the element, and the pages it is on, each with its own <b>👁 Show on page</b> that opens that one. The line reading "Expected: A different photo in each place" is gone — it told you nothing and was sitting where the places belonged.',
