    what: 'Two items that were never the client’s to fix.<br><br><b>The favicon.</b> It is one setting for the whole website — but it was being checked page by page <b>and device by device</b>. Duda serves the mobile version as separate HTML and usually leaves the favicon link out of it, so a website with a perfectly good favicon was told <b>No favicon on this page</b> on Mobile, on every page it has. One website showed it on <b>19 pages</b>. It is now settled once for the website: if <b>any</b> page on <b>any</b> device carries an icon, nothing is reported — and when there genuinely isn’t one, it is a <b>single</b> item that says so, not one per page. The home screen icon works the same way.<br><br><b>Our footer badge.</b> The agency logo we put in the footer was being audited like one of the client’s images, so its alt text read as a business name that isn’t theirs. Any image inside <span class="mono">id="footer-logo"</span> — and the same under <span class="mono">agency-logo</span> or <span class="mono">credit-logo</span>, by id or class — is now skipped by every alt check, never sent to the AI, and never counted as a repeated photo. The client’s own images beside it are checked exactly as before.',
